Everybody called her Aspirin -- she always looked like she was aspiring to become a special state of being, and just looking at her made your headache go away.

She lived on the fourth floor of a building that had been a bakery in 1911 and a laundromat in 1974, and by the time she moved in she was, for all intents and purposes, a pharmacy. The apartment smelled of something clean and faintly medicinal, the way a room smells after someone has been there a long time and has decided to leave it tidy. There was a shelf of amber bottles by the window, a stack of folded towels on the radiator, and a chair by the door that was always, without exception, pulled out and waiting.

The neighbors learned the rules slowly, the way you learn the rules of a house you didn't build.

Don't knock. Knocking was for people who had something to say, and she had never once needed to say anything.

Don't bring flowers. She had a plant, a small one in a chipped blue pot, and she watered it every Sunday morning with the same care she gave to the mail.

Don't ask her name. She had never given one, and she had never been asked, and the silence between those two facts was the most comfortable thing in the building.

The first winter she arrived, the building had a problem. It was the kind of problem that starts in the boiler room and ends in the stairwell, a slow accumulation of small grievances: the radiator that clicked like a dying clock, the hallway light that flickered on the fourth floor, the smell of the bakery that had never quite left the walls. And then there was Mrs. Okonkwo, who lived in 2B, and who had been coughing since October.

Mrs. Okonkwo was a woman of seventy-one with a voice like a page turning and a cough that arrived without warning. She had lived in the building for thirty years and had never once been seen at the window. The building had a rule about windows, an unwritten one, and she had never broken it.

One Tuesday, the coughing moved into the stairwell.

It was a dry, rattling sound, and it came up the stairs in waves, and it stopped people in the middle of their walks. The man in 3A stopped. The woman in 1C stopped. The boy who lived with his grandmother in 2A stopped. They all stood in the stairwell, holding their coats, listening to a woman they had never met cough in the hallway.

Aspirin came out of her apartment. She had her shoes on, which was unusual, and she had a towel over her shoulder, which was more unusual. She walked down the stairs slowly, and she did not look at anyone. She did not look at the man in 3A, who had been staring at her since the third step. She went to the door of 2B and knocked.

Mrs. Okonkwo opened it, and the coughing stopped, and then it started again, and then it stopped, and then it started again, and then it stopped.

"You're late," Mrs. Okonkwo said.

"I'm on time," Aspirin said. "I'm just early."

She went in. She did not ask permission. She did not ask how she was feeling. She took the coat off the hook, put it on the radiator, and sat down on the floor beside the bed, and she began to work.

The neighbors watched from the stairwell, and they watched the way you watch a stranger do something you have never seen done before. Aspirin did not have a bag. She did not have a kit. She had a towel, and a small bottle of something amber, and a pair of scissors, and she moved with a precision that made the man in 3A feel like he had been holding his breath for an hour.

She worked for eleven minutes.

When she came out, the coughing had stopped. Mrs. Okonkwo was sitting on the edge of the bed, and she was holding a cup of tea, and she was looking at the wall.

"You're late," Mrs. Okonkwo said again.

"I'm on time," Aspirin said. "I'm just early."

She went back up the stairs. She did not look at anyone. She did not look at the woman in 1C, who had been crying since the second floor. She went to her door and she opened it, and she stood there for a moment, and then she closed it.

The building changed that night.

It is hard to describe what changed, because it was not one thing. It was the way that people stopped talking in the stairwell. It was the way that the man in 3A started leaving his door open. It was the way that the woman in 1C started watering her plants. It was the way that the boy in 2A started telling his grandmother things he had never told anyone.

They all felt better. They all felt better in a way that had nothing to do with medicine and everything to do with the fact that someone had been there.

The next winter, the building had a different problem. It was a boy in 3B who had been crying in the stairwell for three days, and who had stopped eating, and who had started apologizing to the radiator.

Aspirin went down the stairs. She did not knock. She did not bring a towel. She brought a chair, and she brought a book, and she brought a cup of tea, and she sat down on the floor beside the boy, and she read to him.

She read for four hours.

When she came out, the boy was asleep. He had not eaten in three days, and he had not spoken to anyone in three days, and he had not cried in three days. He was asleep, and he was warm, and he was holding the book.

The neighbors watched from the stairwell, and they watched the way you watch a stranger do something you have never seen done before.

The next winter, the building had a different problem. It was a woman in 1A who had been sitting in the stairwell for a week, and who had stopped going to work, and who had started apologizing to the radiator.

Aspirin went down the stairs. She did not knock. She did not bring a towel. She brought a chair, and she brought a book, and she brought a cup of tea, and she sat down on the floor beside the woman, and she read to her.

She read for six hours.

When she came out, the woman was asleep. She had not eaten in a week, and she had not spoken to anyone in a week, and she had not cried in a week. She was asleep, and she was warm, and she was holding the book.

The neighbors watched from the stairwell, and they watched the way you watch a stranger do something you have never seen done before.

And then one day, in the middle of a summer, the building had a problem that was not a person.

It was the boiler.

The boiler had been making a noise for a month, and then it stopped making a noise, and then it stopped working, and then the building was cold. It was the coldest it had been in thirty years, and the radiator in 2B clicked like a dying clock, and the hallway light flickered on the fourth floor, and the smell of the bakery that had never quite left the walls was gone.

The neighbors went to the boiler room. They went to the boiler room because they had nowhere else to go. They went to the boiler room because they had been cold for a month and they had been cold for a month and they had been cold for a month.

They found the boiler room empty.

They found the boiler room empty and they found the door open, and they found the radiator cold, and they found the smell of the bakery gone, and they found the building cold, and they found the building cold, and they found the building cold.

They went up the stairs. They went up the stairs because they had nowhere else to go. They went up the stairs because they had been cold for a month and they had been cold for a month and they had been cold for a month.

